<!--
    repipe-binary-maze-pointer-becomes: "Binary maze pointer becomes an empty
    string literal"
    docs/re-needs/binary-maze-pointer-becomes.md

    A packed 585-byte maze at 0x403550 opens with a row of zero bytes.  The
    function that walks it takes the maze's address in a variable the type
    lattice had already given `char *`, so the printer replaced the address with
    the string literal at it - and the literal stops at the first byte, which is
    the NUL.  Both pointers into the maze printed `= ""`, and 0x403550 appeared
    nowhere in the emitted C while the 0x403511 and 0x403799 constants around it
    survived.

    P9 emission.  `PrintC::pushConstant`'s pointer arm hands a character-printable
    pointer constant to `pushPtrCharConstant`, which renders the readonly bytes at
    the address instead of the address.  Its gate is `StringManager::isString`,
    and that answers yes for ANY readonly location whose first byte is a NUL:
    `checkCharacters` walks only as far as the first terminator, so for a
    zero-length string it validates zero characters and rejects nothing.

    Fix: `option emptystrconst` (default on) declines the literal when the escaped
    literal would carry no characters at all, and the constant falls through to
    the ordinary casted-hex print.  A literal with at least one character, however
    short, is untouched.

    Not every empty literal is wrong, and the discrimination is the whole point.
    `setlocale(6,"")` is idiomatic C, and a linker that merges string constants
    stores the program's only `""` as the tail NUL of another literal, so an
    empty literal that sits inside a run of string data is REAL.  What the maze
    has instead is a zero row followed by binary: the sixteen bytes at the
    address are the evidence, and only that second shape is declined.

    Source (as(1), x86-64, linked -Ttext=0x100000; `use` at 0x100027 and `use2`
    at 0x100028, both declared `void (char *)` so the three constants are typed
    char *):

        f:  sub  $0x8,%rsp
            mov  $0x100100,%edi     ; readonly: zero row, then blob bytes
            call use
            mov  $0x100110,%edi     ; readonly: "Hi"
            call use
            mov  $0x100120,%edi     ; readonly: a GENUINE "" inside a string run
            call use2
            add  $0x8,%rsp
            ret
        use:  ret
        use2: ret

    Pass 1 (default): assertion #1 proves the blob pointer keeps its address, #2
    that the ordinary two-character literal next to it is untouched, and #3 that
    the genuine empty literal still prints as `""`.  Pass 2 (emptystrconst off):
    assertion #4 reproduces the filed bug, and #3's max pins that the two empty
    literals are told apart rather than both suppressed.
-->
